INT. APPELLATE COURTROOM - LOBBY - DAY

A marble anteroom, high-windowed, quiet as a chapel. A bench, a water cooler, a brass door that gives onto the courtroom proper.

CASSIUS WELLINGTON-OBI stands at the window with his back to the room, a man of fifty with a brief clutched against his chest. ADA LOVELACE-REYES sits on the bench with her ankles crossed, composed in a blue suit. Between them, a younger associate, THEO SANDOVAL, shifts from foot to foot.

She does not look up when he speaks. He doesn't turn when she answers.

THEO
Ms. Lovelace-Reyes, the panel's been out forty minutes.

ADA
Forty-one.

CASSIUS
Forty-two.

THEO
That's a good sign, right? Long means they're reading everything.

ADA
Long means they're arguing.

CASSIUS
Long means nothing.

THEO
So it's a bad sign.

ADA
It's not a sign.

He wipes his palms on his trousers. She straightens a cuff. He turns from the window at last and takes a single step into the room.

CASSIUS
Ada, when you're done being calm, I'd appreciate it if you'd tell me what it is we've forgotten.

ADA
We've forgotten nothing.

CASSIUS
Then why am I sweating?

THEO
Because the AC's broken.

The brass door swings open. A clerk looks out and calls them in. The three of them stand together. Nobody moves toward the door until Ada does.
